# Swift / SwiftUI (iOS)

Caps: soft **250** / hard **400** lines per file; functions **≤50** lines. Split views before growing past hard cap.

## Principles (Context7 / Apple-aligned)

- Prefer modern `@Observable` models owned with `@State`; mark observable classes `@MainActor`
- View-owned state (`@State`, `@AppStorage`, env reads) is `private`; inputs (`let`, `@Binding`, `@Bindable`) are not
- Decompose large views so state changes only re-evaluate affected subviews; use child `View` types or `Equatable` views for expensive branches
- Prefer granular dependencies for list rows; avoid stuffing frequently changing values into `Environment`
- Use `LazyVStack` / `LazyHStack` / `List` for long collections
- Keep `body` declarative: side effects in `.task` / `.onChange`, not in computed layout

## Structure

- Screens in `Views/`; reusable UI in `Components/`; networking/persistence in `Services/`; models in `Models/`
- One screen (or tightly related helpers) per file; extract rows, sheets, and form sections into sibling files when approaching soft cap
- Prefer value types (`struct`) for models; `final class` only for shared services / observables

## Do / Don't

```swift
// ❌ God view with mixed state + networking inline
// ✅ Screen owns UI state; repository/service owns I/O; child views own isolated UI

struct RowView: View {
    let title: String
    @Binding var isOn: Bool
    @State private var expanded = false  // private view state
    var body: some View { /* ≤50 lines or extract */ }
}
```

- No force-unwraps in production paths; use `guard` / `if let` / `Result`
- Avoid `AnyView` unless type erasure is required
- Match existing Theme/Components APIs; do not invent parallel design tokens
